Loops are one of the biggest foundations in programming because they let your program do something repeatedly without you manually writing the same code over and over.

Without loops, real applications would basically be impossible to build efficiently.

Every major application uses loops constantly:

* social media feeds
* notifications
* databases
* APIs
* dashboards
* games
* admin panels
* React rendering
* Rails data processing

All of them rely heavily on looping.

---

# What a Loop Really Means

A loop is basically:

```text id="x9e0u1"
“Keep doing this until some condition changes.”
```

That’s all it is.

---

# Real Project Examples

---

# Example 1 — Displaying Database Records

You practiced:

```ruby id="r0t2hv"
while number <= 10
```

Real Rails equivalent:

```ruby id="34w8jr"
@jobs.each do |job|
  p job.title
end
```

Imagine your Job Tracker app.

If a user has:

* 50 job applications
* 200 job applications
* 1000 job applications

you cannot manually write:

```ruby id="97j8d3"
p job1.title
p job2.title
p job3.title
```

A loop handles all of them automatically.

That’s one of the biggest real-world uses of loops.

---

# Example 2 — Rendering React Components

In React:

```javascript id="5dlo7c"
jobs.map((job) => (
  <JobCard title={job.title} />
))
```

That’s ALSO looping.

It loops through job data and renders components.

So when you learn loops in Ruby, you’re also strengthening your understanding of:

* JavaScript loops
* React rendering
* API data handling

---

# Example 3 — User Input Validation

You practiced:

```ruby id="8w5z0j"
while true
```

Real project example:

```ruby id="ql73zj"
while password.length < 8
  p "Password too short"
  password = gets.chomp
end
```

Meaning:

* keep asking until valid input is given

This is extremely common in software.

